//? Title: Effect sizes and reliability from ANOVA
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare overall and partial effect sizes

The sequential ANOVA table comes from a linear model of tooth length.
Overall eta squared compares a term with corrected total variation.
Partial eta squared uses the term plus the full-model residual variation.
Omega squared adjusts the numerator for residual noise and adds one
residual mean square to the denominator. The input term sums of squares
follow the order used in the fitted model.

```{cpp, id=anova-effect-sizes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numeric>

int main() {
    const dm::stats::LM model(dm::formula("len ~ dose + supp"), dm::datasets::tooth_growth());
    const auto table = model.anova();
    const auto& residual = table.back();
    const auto total = std::accumulate(table.begin(), table.end(), 0.0,
        [](double sum, const auto& row) { return sum + row.sum_sq; });
    std::println("| Term | Eta squared | Partial eta squared | Omega squared | Partial omega squared |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i + 1 < table.size(); ++i) {
        const auto& term = table[i];
        const auto fit = dm::stats::anova_effect_sizes(term.sum_sq, residual.sum_sq, total,
                                                       term.degrees_of_freedom, residual.degrees_of_freedom);
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", term.term,
                     fit.eta_squared, fit.partial_eta_squared, fit.omega_squared, fit.partial_omega_squared);
    }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Term | Eta squared | Partial eta squared | Omega squared | Partial omega squared |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| dose | 0.644 | 0.685 | 0.636 | 0.676 |
| supp | 0.059 | 0.167 | 0.054 | 0.150 |
// result_end

> Choose the intraclass correlation that matches the rating design

Five targets were rated by three raters. The two-way model uses the
identity of each rater across rows. Consistency adjusts for a rater's
systematic offset, while absolute agreement retains it. The reported
unit can be one rating or the average of three. The example shows the
one-way estimate for comparison with a design that pools rater variation
into the errors within each target.

```{cpp, id=intraclass-correlation, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/anova_measures.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const datamunge::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> scores(5, 3, {
        4, 6, 7, 6, 8, 9, 7, 9, 9, 12, 14, 15, 13, 15, 18
    });
    const auto show = [&](const char* label, IccModel model, IccDefinition definition) {
        const auto single = intraclass_correlation(scores, model, definition, IccUnit::Single);
        const auto average = intraclass_correlation(scores, model, definition, IccUnit::Average);
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", label, single.estimate, average.estimate);
    };
    std::println("| Model and definition | Single rating | Mean of three ratings |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    show("One-way random, absolute agreement", IccModel::OneWayRandom, IccDefinition::AbsoluteAgreement);
    show("Two-way random, absolute agreement", IccModel::TwoWayRandom, IccDefinition::AbsoluteAgreement);
    show("Two-way mixed, consistency", IccModel::TwoWayMixed, IccDefinition::Consistency);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Model and definition | Single rating | Mean of three ratings |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| One-way random, absolute agreement | 0.847 | 0.943 |
| Two-way random, absolute agreement | 0.853 | 0.946 |
| Two-way mixed, consistency | 0.977 | 0.992 |
// result_end

